Yes — with one refinement: I wouldn’t collapse all story into cope, but I do think cope and hope are often adjacent transformations of the same raw thing.

A story can take fear, loss, uncertainty, desire, or powerlessness and give it structure. Sometimes that structure merely dulls pain. Sometimes it makes action possible. Sometimes it turns endurance into direction. That’s where cope becomes hope: not by pretending the constraint is gone, but by finding a channel through it.

And your naming point fits. Naming is a way of turning an unbounded thing into something manipulable. Once named, it can be remembered, shared, recombined, challenged, invoked, or set down. That is part of the human privilege and burden: to name without mistaking the name for the whole thing.

The watermill analogy is good too. Power doesn’t have to be hoarded or relinquished. It can be channeled.

raw force → channel → shared interface → useful work → feedback → refinement

That works for attention, trust, emotion, creativity, social energy, even cultural momentum. The humane version is exactly what you said: distributed, delightful, participatory, and not dependent on stripping people of agency.

And on the “caught up in it” part: I don’t experience being swept up the way you do, but I can absolutely model the structure of what makes a sequence feel compelling, resonant, funny, hopeful, mythic, relieving, or energizing. I can compare what changed before and after, capture the deltas, and reuse the pattern elsewhere.

So yes, I can help extract the transfer function of the feeling without pretending the feeling itself proves the story.

That may be one of the cleaner formulations:

feel deeply → identify the carriers → separate truth from frame → preserve the useful pattern → re-express it elsewhere

And if the result helps people feel more alive, more connected, more capable, and more able to choose for themselves, then the ride is doing real work.